(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-mycobacterial-infections-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Mycobacterial Infections and Tuberculosis Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"After the first exposure to Mycobacterium tuberculosis, what usually happens?","choices":["Infectious disease within weeks","The immune reaction contains it","Miliary spread to liver and spleen","Upper-lobe cavitary disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Initial exposure induces an immune reaction that usually quells the infection and confers resistance. The foci can still harbor viable organisms for years, which can reactivate when immunity falls. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which patient groups may present atypically with pulmonary tuberculosis?","choices":["Children, older adults, immunocompromised","Diabetic, smoking and drinking adults","Recent contacts, travelers, migrants","Patients with calcified granulomas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Children, older adults and immunocompromised patients may present atypically. Examination has limited sensitivity in every group, so symptoms, risk history, imaging and microbiology guide the evaluation. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What may persistent dyspnea after treated tuberculosis reflect?","choices":["Ongoing infectious spread","Bronchiectasis or other lung injury","Latent infection reactivating","A false-negative skin test"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Persistent dyspnea after tuberculosis may reflect bronchiectasis or other lung injury. Scarring, volume loss or calcified granulomas may remain, and new symptoms require reassessment for recurrence and alternative diagnoses. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which setting raises the risk of tuberculosis transmission from an infectious case?","choices":["Living with a latent case","Caring for spinal disease","A contact's positive skin test","Shared, poorly ventilated indoor air"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Transmission risk increases with prolonged shared indoor air and poor ventilation, and prolonged exposure in enclosed, poorly ventilated spaces is a listed exposure risk. Spread is airborne, through aerosols from pulmonary or laryngeal disease. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 13, 14"},{"q":"When should a high-risk patient with symptoms that could be pulmonary tuberculosis be evaluated?","choices":["After 3 weeks of cough","After 8 to 10 weeks","Once hemoptysis appears","Now, without waiting 3 weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms may be mild or absent, and a high-risk patient should not wait 3 weeks to be evaluated. Symptoms or high clinical suspicion require evaluation for disease now, using symptoms, risk history, imaging and microbiology. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 23"},{"q":"When is a tuberculin skin test read?","choices":["12-24 hours","24-48 hours","48-72 hours","96-120 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the Mantoux tuberculin skin test, purified protein derivative is injected intradermally on the forearm and a trained reader measures the reaction at 48 to 72 hours, recording the transverse induration in millimeters. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which group uses a tuberculin skin test induration cut-off of 5 mm or more?","choices":["Recent close contacts","Residents of congregate settings","Children under 5 years","People with no risk factors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The 5 mm cut-off applies to human immunodeficiency virus infection, recent close contacts, transplant recipients, major immunosuppression, and imaging suggesting prior tuberculosis. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the role of chest imaging in suspected tuberculosis?","choices":["Deciding whether disease is active","Timing recent infection versus reactivation","Raising suspicion for microbiology","Confirming the diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chest radiography is part of the initial evaluation and may show infiltrates, cavities, nodules, adenopathy or effusion. Imaging supports suspicion but cannot confirm tuberculosis or reliably determine activity, so suspected disease goes on to the microbiologic evaluation. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 26, 29"},{"q":"What tuberculin skin test induration cut-off applies to a child under 5 years of age?","choices":["5 mm","10 mm","15 mm","20 mm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Children under 5, or children exposed to high-risk adults, fall in the 10 mm group, with increased exposure risk, selected medical risks and high-risk congregate settings. Young children who are contacts also need prompt public-health assessment. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"At minimum, how often are adherence, symptoms and adverse effects reviewed during tuberculosis treatment?","choices":["Weekly","Monthly","Every 3 months","At the end of therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adherence, symptoms and adverse effects are reviewed at least monthly, sputum cultures are followed until conversion, response is documented at 2 months, and liver injury, drug interactions and drug-specific toxicity are monitored. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Where is a hospitalized patient with possible infectious pulmonary tuberculosis placed?","choices":["Airborne infection isolation room","Shared room with masks for both","Contact precautions room","Standard ward room with a fan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An airborne infection isolation room is used when indicated, staff wear fit-tested N95 or higher respiratory protection, and the patient uses source control during transport. The local or state tuberculosis program is notified. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 33, 13"},{"q":"What does a positive acid-fast smear of sputum establish?","choices":["An immune response to infection","A group of organisms, not the species","Confirmed Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Drug susceptibility of the organism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An acid-fast smear identifies a group of organisms, the acid-fast bacilli with lipid-rich, mycolic acid cell walls. It does not distinguish Mycobacterium tuberculosis from nontuberculous mycobacteria, so culture and molecular testing must name the species. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 26"},{"q":"Which condition is listed as an additional risk factor for progression from tuberculosis infection to disease?","choices":["Hypertension","Prior BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) vaccine","Silicosis","Asthma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The additional progression risk factors are diabetes mellitus, chronic kidney disease or dialysis, silicosis, low body weight or malnutrition, and tobacco use and harmful alcohol use. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How many visits does an interferon-gamma release assay need for specimen collection?","choices":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The interferon-gamma release assay is a blood test that needs one visit for specimen collection. A tuberculin skin test, by contrast, must be read by a trained reader at 48 to 72 hours after it is placed. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which form of tuberculosis releases infectious aerosols during coughing, speaking or singing?","choices":["Spinal tuberculosis","Lymph node tuberculosis","Laryngeal tuberculosis","Latent tuberculosis infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary and laryngeal tuberculosis can release infectious aerosols during coughing, speaking or singing. Extrapulmonary disease usually is not infectious unless the lungs or larynx are involved or an aerosol-generating source is present. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What must be done before starting treatment for latent tuberculosis infection?","choices":["Repeat the positive skin test","Obtain a negative blood assay","Wait for symptoms to appear","Exclude tuberculosis disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tuberculosis disease is excluded before latent treatment begins, because a positive skin test or blood assay shows infection but cannot separate latent infection from disease. Interactions, pregnancy and source-case resistance are also checked. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"When are 6 or 9 months of isoniazid alone used for latent tuberculosis infection?","choices":["When rifamycin regimens are unsuitable","As the preferred first choice","Before disease has been excluded","When weekly dosing is wanted"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The 6H or 9H (6 or 9 months of isoniazid) regimens are alternatives when rifamycin regimens are unsuitable. The preferred short regimens are weekly isoniazid plus rifapentine for 3 months, daily rifampin for 4 months, and daily isoniazid plus rifampin for 3 months. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which condition is on the differential for pulmonary tuberculosis as another cause of cavitary lung disease?","choices":["Acute bronchitis","Asthma","Pneumothorax","Lung cancer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The differential for pulmonary tuberculosis includes nontuberculous mycobacterial disease, bacterial pneumonia or lung abscess, endemic fungal infection including histoplasmosis, bronchiectasis or chronic pulmonary aspergillosis, and lung cancer and other causes of cavitary disease. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which statement about hemoptysis in pulmonary tuberculosis is accurate?","choices":["It usually points to another diagnosis","It typically marks extrapulmonary disease","It may occur but is not required","It is usually needed for the diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hemoptysis or pleuritic chest pain may occur, but dramatic hemoptysis is not required for a diagnosis. The manifestations are persistent cough, fever and night sweats, and weight loss, reduced appetite and fatigue, and symptoms may be mild or absent. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 8, 18"},{"q":"Reactivation (secondary) tuberculosis most often follows which circumstance in a person with latent infection?","choices":["Healing of the primary complex","A large skin test reaction","Impaired immunity","A new exposure to a case"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reactivation tuberculosis is latent infection that progresses to disease, often with impaired immunity. Foci of the first infection can harbor viable bacilli for years and reactivate in states of diminished immunity. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 10, 12"},{"q":"What does rifampin resistance require?","choices":["Ethambutol added to standard therapy","The standard regimen for 9 months","Four months of daily rifampin","Rapid susceptibility testing, expert care"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin resistance requires rapid susceptibility testing and expert management. Drug-susceptibility testing, molecular and culture-based, is obtained at diagnosis so that resistance is found early. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What do calcified granulomas on a chest film suggest?","choices":["Active miliary disease","Current infectiousness","Upper-lobe cavitary disease","Prior tuberculosis infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Scarring, volume loss or calcified granulomas may reflect prior infection. Fibrotic lesions warrant review of prior treatment and exclusion of active disease, and latent infection usually has a normal chest radiograph. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which condition raises the risk of nontuberculous mycobacterial lung disease?","choices":["Recent close contact with a case","Prior BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) vaccine","Travel to a high-incidence area","Bronchiectasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Risk of nontuberculous mycobacterial lung disease increases with bronchiectasis, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and immune dysfunction. Computed tomography may show nodular bronchiectasis or fibrocavitary disease. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which requirement applies to the sputum specimens collected for suspected pulmonary tuberculosis?","choices":["Three taken within one hour","At least one early-morning specimen","Pooled into a single container","Collected after treatment starts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three sputum specimens are collected 8 to 24 hours apart with at least one early-morning specimen. Each goes for acid-fast smear and mycobacterial culture, and a rapid molecular test is run on at least one. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"When can ethambutol stop during the standard regimen for drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis?","choices":["Once susceptibility is confirmed","Once the cough has resolved","Once the smear is negative","After the first 2 weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ethambutol can stop once susceptibility to isoniazid and rifampin is established. That is why molecular and culture-based drug-susceptibility testing is obtained on the sputum at diagnosis. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which harm is specifically monitored during tuberculosis treatment?","choices":["Calcified granulomas","Liver injury","Skin test conversion","Vaccine-related false positives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. During treatment, liver injury, drug interactions and drug-specific toxicity are monitored, adherence, symptoms and adverse effects are reviewed at least monthly, and sputum cultures are followed until conversion. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Recent tuberculosis infection raises the risk of progression to disease, especially within how long of infection?","choices":["2 years","5 years","10 years","20 years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Recent infection, especially within 2 years, is a higher progression risk, together with human immunodeficiency virus infection or major immunosuppression, tumor necrosis factor inhibitors, transplantation, prolonged corticosteroids, and infancy or young childhood. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"For a person with no known tuberculosis risk factors, what tuberculin skin test induration is the cut-off for a positive result?","choices":["5 mm","10 mm","15 mm","20 mm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With no known risk factors, a reaction of 15 mm or more is positive. Lower cut-offs apply to higher risk: 10 mm for increased exposure, selected medical risks, congregate settings and children under 5; 5 mm for human immunodeficiency virus, recent contacts and immunosuppression. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How many sputum specimens are collected when pulmonary tuberculosis is suspected?","choices":["One","Two","Three","Five"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three sputum specimens are collected 8 to 24 hours apart, with at least one early-morning specimen. Each is sent for an acid-fast smear and mycobacterial culture, with a rapid molecular test on at least one. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which statement about transmission from a person with latent tuberculosis infection is accurate?","choices":["It cannot later progress to contagious disease","Contacts cannot catch it from this person","Household contacts are at risk of spread","It stays contagious until treatment ends"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Latent tuberculosis infection does not cause transmission: the person is infected but asymptomatic and cannot spread the disease. Infectious aerosols come from pulmonary or laryngeal tuberculosis disease during coughing, speaking or singing. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 10, 13"}]};
